  "question": {
    "text": "A wire of length 10 cm and diameter 0.5 mm is used in a bulb. The temperature of the wire is $1727^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ and power radiated by the wire is 94.2 W. Its emissivity is $\\frac{x}{8}$ where $x=$_______<br/>(Given $\\sigma=6.0 \\times 10^{-8} \\mathrm{~W} \\mathrm{~m}^{-2} \\mathrm{~K}^{-4}, \\pi=3.14$ and assume that the emissivity of wire material is same at all wavelength.)"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "5",
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\mathrm{L}=10 \\mathrm{~cm}, \\mathrm{~d}=0.5 \\mathrm{~mm}, \\mathrm{~T}=1727^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}=2000 \\mathrm{~K}$<br/>Power, $\\mathrm{P}=94.2 \\mathrm{~W}$<br/>$\\mathrm{P}=\\varepsilon \\sigma \\mathrm{AT}^4$<br/>$94.2=\\varepsilon \\times\\left(6 \\times 10^{-8}\\right)(\\pi \\mathrm{dL})(2000)^4$<br/>$94.2=\\varepsilon \\times\\left(6 \\times 10^{-8}\\right)(3.14)(0.5)\\left(10^{-3}\\right)$<br/>$\\left(10 \\times 10^{-2}\\right)(2000)^4$<br/>$\\varepsilon=\\frac{94.2}{(94.2)(16)}=\\frac{5}{8}$"
  }
}